---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import { timeFieldNativeTree, timeFieldTree } from "../../../demos/time-field";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";
import "@skryensya/core/components/time-field.css";

const install = `@import "@skryensya/core/components/button.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/time-field.css";`;


const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

---

<Base
  title="TimeField"
  description="A segmented time field: hour, minute and AM/PM as independently editable parts, operable from the keyboard."
>
  <h1>TimeField</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A field for a local wall-clock time, no date, no time zone. Hour, minute and (in a 12-hour
    locale) AM/PM are three independently editable segments in one field, instead of the chrome of
    a plain <code>&lt;input type="time"&gt;</code>, which differs enough across Chrome, Firefox and
    Safari that it cannot be styled or trusted to look the same twice. There is no
    <code>@zag-js/time-picker</code> machine, so this component owns its state by hand, like Slider
    and Segmented, and there is no popover either: an earlier design put a scroll-wheel picker
    behind a trigger, and it turned out to be neither simpler nor more accessible than building the
    segments directly.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview
    tree={timeFieldTree(t, { locale: "en-US", name: "departure" })}
    label="TimeField"
  />

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <p>
    Segment order and separators come from <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat.formatToParts</code>, not from
    assumptions: some locales put the day period before the time, and the separator is not always
    <code>":"</code>. The public value is still the canonical <code>HH:mm</code> string (the same
    shape a plain <code>&lt;input type="time"&gt;</code> submits), carried on a hidden input, so a
    form behind TimeField never has to parse a locale-dependent string. React holds this as
    component state (<code>value</code>/<code>defaultValue</code>, an <code>onValueChange</code>
    callback). Vanilla hydrates the authored <code>data-sk-time-field</code>: the consumer only
    authors the root and its label, and the component builds the segments from
    <code>data-locale</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Editing a segment</h2>
  <p>
    Typing a digit fills the focused segment and advances as soon as no further digit could keep it
    valid: typing <kbd class="sk-kbd">1</kbd> in a 12-hour hour segment waits briefly for a possible
    second digit (<code>10</code>–<code>12</code>); typing <kbd class="sk-kbd">9</kbd> advances at
    once, because no second digit could follow it and stay ≤ 12. Arrow up/down steps the value and
    wraps at the ends; <kbd class="sk-kbd">Backspace</kbd> clears the segment;
    <kbd class="sk-kbd">←</kbd>/<kbd class="sk-kbd">→</kbd> move between segments; typing the first
    letter of the locale's AM/PM label on that segment sets it directly.
  </p>

  <h2>If you only need the platform picker</h2>
  <p>
    A plain <code>&lt;input type="time"&gt;</code> still works, needs no CSS or JavaScript, and brings
    the OS's own keyboard and picker for free. Reach for TimeField only once that picker's
    inconsistency across browsers, or the total lack of styling hooks, actually costs you something,
    the same decision DatePicker makes against a plain <code>type="date"</code>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={timeFieldNativeTree(t, { name: "departure" })} label="Native time input" />

  <h2>Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    Each segment is <code>role="spinbutton"</code> inside a <code>role="group"</code> named by the
    field's label: <code>aria-valuenow</code>/<code>aria-valuetext</code> carry its current value (a
    friendly placeholder like "hh" before anything is set, not an empty string), and
    <code>aria-valuemin</code>/<code>aria-valuemax</code> its real range: 1–12 for an hour segment
    in a 12-hour locale, 0–23 in a 24-hour one. Each segment is its own tab stop, the way a native
    multi-part date input already works, so keyboard use needs nothing beyond Tab and the arrows
    documented above.
  </p>

  <h2>CSS</h2>
  <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
  <StylingHooks
    groups={[
      { file: "components/time-field.css", probe: "sk-time-field", title: "TimeField" },
      { file: "components/button.css", probe: "sk-button", title: "Inherited from Button" },
    ]}
  />
</Base>
